Q: How can I get global keystrokes without eating all the keys? A: <p>Luckily, my requirements were flexible enough that I could use the callback approach from Xtst (adapted from <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/73771689/29046053">here</a>), but actually getting the <code>XKeyEvents</code> from there required two more includes and quite a bit of work:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;X11/Xlib.h&gt; // Was already present.
#include &lt;X11/Xlibint.h&gt;
#include &lt;X11/extensions/record.h&gt;

Display *display;

static void eventCallback(XPointer, XRecordInterceptData *data);

int main(const int argc, const char *argv[]) {
    display = XOpenDisplay(nullptr);
    if (!display) {
        return 1;
    }
    const Window window = XDefaultRootWindow(display);
    if (!window) {
        return 2;
    }

    XRecordRange *rr = XRecordAllocRange();
    if (!rr) {
        return 3;
    }
    rr-&gt;device_events.first = 2; // First possible event
    rr-&gt;device_events.last = LASTEvent;
    XRecordClientSpec rcs = XRecordAllClients;
    XRecordContext ctx = XRecordCreateContext(display, 0, &amp;rcs, 1, &amp;rr, 1);
    if (!ctx) {
        return 4;
    }
    XFree(rr);

    // Next statement hangs until we're done processing input.
    if (!XRecordEnableContext(display, ctx, eventCallback, nullptr)) {
        return 5;
    }
    return 0;
}

typedef union XRecordDatum {
    unsigned char type;
    xEvent event;
    xResourceReq req;
    xGenericReply reply;
    xError error;
    xConnSetupPrefix setup;
} XRecordDatum;

static XMappingEvent convertMappingEvent(xEvent tst_event) {
    return (XMappingEvent){
        .type = tst_event.u.u.type,
        .display = display,
        .request = tst_event.u.mappingNotify.request,
        .first_keycode = tst_event.u.mappingNotify.firstKeyCode,
        .count = tst_event.u.mappingNotify.count
    };
}

static XKeyEvent convertKeyEvent(xEvent tst_event) {
    return (XKeyEvent){
        .type = tst_event.u.u.type,
        .display = display,
        .window = tst_event.u.focus.window,
        .root = tst_event.u.keyButtonPointer.root,
        .subwindow = tst_event.u.keyButtonPointer.child,
        .time = tst_event.u.keyButtonPointer.time,
        .x = tst_event.u.keyButtonPointer.eventX,
        .y = tst_event.u.keyButtonPointer.eventY,
        .x_root = tst_event.u.keyButtonPointer.rootX,
        .y_root = tst_event.u.keyButtonPointer.rootY,
        .state = tst_event.u.keyButtonPointer.state,
        .keycode = tst_event.u.u.detail,
        .same_screen = tst_event.u.keyButtonPointer.sameScreen
    };
}

static void eventCallback(XPointer, XRecordInterceptData *data) {
    if (data-&gt;category != XRecordFromServer) {
        goto post_call;
    }
    const XRecordDatum datum = *(XRecordDatum *)data-&gt;data;

    switch (datum.type) {
        case MappingNotify: {
            XMappingEvent me = convertMappingEvent(datum.event);
            /* ... */
            [[fallthrough]];
        }
        default:
            goto post_call;
        case KeyPress: case KeyRelease:
    }

    XKeyEvent ke = convertKeyEvent(datum.event);
    /* ... */

post_call:
    XRecordFreeData(data);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Xtst gives us lowercase <code>xEvents</code>, and we want capital <code>XEvents</code> (yes, amazing convention), so the <code>convertEvent</code> functions convert the former to the latter. If you need more or different events, you'll need to write conversion functions for them, too. Anyway, this process gets keys even if other applications have them grabbed and doesn't eat them itself.</p>
<p>However, this uses the Xtst convention of defining a callback function instead of the Xlib convention of waiting for events. In my case, this is fine, but that won't work for all cases.</p>

Q: Writing 1 million lines of data to a single file, in Java. Then re-write with appended data A: <h2>tl;dr</h2>
<p>Use features of modern Java. No need for third-party library.</p>
<p>Original file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Files.write(                                     // Write a `List` of `CharSequence` objects (such as `String`) into a file. Defaults to UTF-8 character encoding. 
    Paths.get( &quot;/path/to/example.txt&quot; ) ,        // Returns a `Path` object. 
    IntStream                                    // A stream of `int` values. 
        .range( 0 , 1_000_000 )                  // Generate a series of numbers from zero (inclusive) to a million (exclusive). Acts like a `for` loop here, to iterate a million times. We ignore each particular `int` value.
        .mapToObj( index -&gt; UUID.randomUUID() )  // For each number, generate a Version 4 UUID (mostly random content). 
        .map( UUID :: toString )                 // Generate text representing each of those UUID objects, in canonical hex format. 
        .toList()                                // Collect all those `String` objects into a `List`. 
)                                                // Automatically closes file. 
</code></pre>
<p>Rewritten file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Files.write(
    Paths.get( &quot;/path/to/example.txt&quot; ) ,
    Files
        .readAllLines( Paths.get( &quot;/path/to/example.txt&quot; ) )
        .stream()
        .map( line -&gt; 
            line
                .concat( &quot;|&quot; )
                .concat( Instant.now().toString() )
    )
    .toList()
)
</code></pre>
<h2>Modern Java</h2>
<p>Let's try using features of modern Java such as NIO.2, streams, lambdas, and method references. We will test elapsed time.</p>
<h2>Write file</h2>
<p>Generate one million <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universally_unique_identifier" rel="nofollow noreferrer">UUID</a> values, represented as text in canonical hexadecimal format.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>List &lt; String &gt; uuids = IntStream.range( 0 , 1_000_000 ).mapToObj( index -&gt; UUID.randomUUID() ).map( UUID :: toString ).toList();
</code></pre>
<p>Write those to a file. We pass our list of <code>String</code> objects to <code>Files.write</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>try { Files.write( path , uuids ); }
catch ( IOException e ) { e.printStackTrace(); }
</code></pre>
<p>Data snippet:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>b541cd3c-60d2-4b11-b7da-7f21a5c18bea
a3446075-5bbd-42ab-a9b4-a154f8190900
9144b23b-de0e-41a8-8285-b0b0dd8ccbe6
…
</code></pre>
<h2>Manipulate data</h2>
<p>You said you need to re-write the file by appending more data. Let's append a delimiter of <code>|</code> (pipe character) and the current moment. We manipulate the data in memory, then write back out to the same file.</p>
<p>Manipulate the data.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>lines = lines.stream().map( line -&gt; line.concat( &quot;|&quot; ).concat( Instant.now().toString() ) ).toList();
</code></pre>
<h2>Rewrite file</h2>
<p>Rewrite to the same file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>try { Files.write( path , lines ); }
catch ( IOException e ) { e.printStackTrace(); }
</code></pre>
<h2>Example code</h2>
<p>Pulling all that code together looks like the following.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class RewriteBigFile
{
    static void main ( )
    {
        IO.println( Runtime.version() );
        long start = System.nanoTime();

        // Input data. Generate one million UUID values, represented as text in canonical hexadecimal format.
        List &lt; String &gt; uuids = IntStream.range( 0 , 1_000_000 ).mapToObj( index -&gt; UUID.randomUUID() ).map( UUID :: toString ).toList();

        // Write file.
        final String fileName = &quot;/path/to/example.txt&quot;;
        final Path path = Paths.get( fileName );

        try { Files.write( path , uuids ); }
        catch ( IOException e ) { e.printStackTrace(); }

        // Read file.
        List &lt; String &gt; lines;
        try { lines = Files.readAllLines( path ); }
        catch ( IOException e ) { throw new RuntimeException( e ); }

        // Manipulate data.
        lines = lines.stream().map( line -&gt; line.concat( &quot;|&quot; ).concat( Instant.now().toString() ) ).toList();

        // Rewrite file.
        try { Files.write( path , lines ); }
        catch ( IOException e ) { e.printStackTrace(); }

        Duration duration = Duration.ofNanos( System.nanoTime() - start );
        IO.println( &quot;duration = &quot; + duration );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Data snippet:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>b541cd3c-60d2-4b11-b7da-7f21a5c18bea|2026-04-02T04:56:14.755410Z
a3446075-5bbd-42ab-a9b4-a154f8190900|2026-04-02T04:56:14.756789Z
9144b23b-de0e-41a8-8285-b0b0dd8ccbe6|2026-04-02T04:56:14.756800Z
…
</code></pre>
<p>On <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacOS_Sequoia" rel="nofollow noreferrer">macOS Sequoia</a> 15.7.4, on a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacBook_Pro_(Apple_silicon)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MacBook Pro</a> with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_M1#M1_Pro" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Apple M1 Pro</a> chip, with built-in <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solid-state_drive" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SSD</a> storage, running <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_version_history#Java_SE_26" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Java 26</a>, all of this writing, manipulating , and rewriting takes less than one second.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>26+35
duration = PT0.777597417S
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>In real work, you might not want to risk losing your data while re-writing to the same file. You likely would write to a second file, then delete the original <em>after</em> completion.</p>
<p>Also, in real work, I would add some validation such as nulls and empty strings. And I would likely use <em>JSpecify</em> annotations to enable null-checking tools.</p>

Q: Write the contents of Arraylist to txt file in Java, each line with a group of list elements A: <h2>Stream Gatherers</h2>
<p>This is a good use-case for the <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/485" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Gatherers</a> feature in Streams in Java 24+.</p>
<p>With gatherers we can define our own intermediate operations by implementing the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/stream/Gatherer.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Gatherer</code></a> interface. The <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/stream/Gatherers.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Gatherers</code></a> utility class provides a few helpful <code>Gatherer</code> implementations.</p>
<p>One of those implementations is <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/stream/Gatherers.html#windowFixed(int)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>windowFixed</code></a> which groups a number of elements (a “window”) for treatment together. The treatment we want is to make a combined string of our <code>Double</code> objects’ value, separated by COMMA characters.</p>
<h3>Example code</h3>
<p>First, your sample data. Apparently you intended to mix integers with fractional numbers. But a Java collection must have objects of the same type. So we cannot mix <code>Integer</code> or <code>Long</code> with <code>Float</code> or <code>Double</code> objects. As a workaround, we can use their shared superclass <code>Number</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>ArrayList &lt; Number &gt; numbers =
        new ArrayList &lt;&gt;(
                List.of(
                        1 , 123_456_789 , 1.222 , 2.333 ,
                        2 , 9_876_541 , 4.555 , 7.888
                )
        );
</code></pre>
<p>Next we instantiate a <code>Gatherer</code> to work with groups of four elements at a time. Each group is joined together into a single COMMA-delimited piece of text. We gather those pieces of text into list. We will treat this list as a collection of lines to be written to a file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>List &lt; String &gt; lines =
        numbers
                .stream()
                .map( String :: valueOf )
                .gather( Gatherers.windowFixed( 4 ) ) // ⬅️ Gatherers.
                .map( window -&gt;
                        String.join(
                                &quot;, &quot; ,
                                window.stream().map( Object :: toString ).toList()  // Make a `List` of our four numbers converted to strings. 
                        )
                )
                .toList();
</code></pre>
<p>Write our lines to a file. The character encoding of the written file defaults to UTF-8. Each line will be terminated by the platform’s <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newline" rel="nofollow noreferrer">line separator</a>, as defined by the system property <code>line.separator</code>. Our <code>List</code> of <code>String</code> objects qualifies as the <code>Iterable&lt;? extends CharSequence</code> we need to pass to <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html#write(java.nio.file.Path,java.lang.Iterable,java.nio.charset.Charset,java.nio.file.OpenOption...)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Files.write</code></a>.</p>
<p>This code is made simple by using Java NIO.2 features such as the <code>Paths</code> &amp; <code>Files</code> utility classes.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Path outputFile = Path.of( &quot;/Users/basil_dot_work/example.txt&quot; );
try
{
    // Write each string on its own line
    Files.write( outputFile , lines ); // Defaults character encoding to UTF-8.
    IO.println( &quot;\nSuccessfully written to: &quot; + outputFile.toAbsolutePath() );
}
catch ( IOException e )
{
    System.err.println( &quot;Error writing to file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
    e.printStackTrace();
}
</code></pre>
<p>When run:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>1, 123456789, 1.222, 2.333
2, 9876541, 4.555, 7.888
</code></pre>
<h3>Full code</h3>
<p>For your copy-paste convenience, let's pull that code all together.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>package work.basil.example.files;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Gatherers;

public class WriteBunchesOfElements
{
    static void main ( )
    {
        ArrayList &lt; Number &gt; numbers =
                new ArrayList &lt;&gt;(
                        List.of(
                                1 , 123_456_789 , 1.222 , 2.333 ,
                                2 , 9_876_541 , 4.555 , 7.888
                        )
                );

// Process the stream: group every 4 numbers into COMMA-separated strings.
        List &lt; String &gt; lines =
                numbers
                        .stream()
                        .map( String :: valueOf )
                        .gather( Gatherers.windowFixed( 4 ) )
                        .map( window -&gt;
                                String.join(
                                        &quot;, &quot; ,
                                        window.stream().map( Object :: toString ).toList()  // Make a `List` of our four numbers converted to strings.
                                )
                        )
                        .toList();

// Write the list to a file using Java NIO.2.
        Path outputFile = Path.of( &quot;/Users/basil_dot_work/example.txt&quot; );
        try
        {
            // Write each string on its own line
            Files.write( outputFile , lines ); // Defaults character encoding to UTF-8.
            IO.println( &quot;\nSuccessfully written to: &quot; + outputFile.toAbsolutePath() );
        }
        catch ( IOException e )
        {
            System.err.println( &quot;Error writing to file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Records</h2>
<p>I answered this Question as asked. But in real work I would have defined a <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/395" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>record</code></a> class like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>record Statistic ( int line , int quantity , double x , double y ) {}
</code></pre>
<p>… to hold each group of four values. This makes our intentions clear with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-documenting_code" rel="nofollow noreferrer">self-documenting</a> code.</p>
<p>After instantiating the record objects to represent our data set, I would write them out to a file. I would likely add a method to the record class to generate data in desired format.</p>
<p>Note that a <code>record</code> class can be defined locally, as well as nested or separately. So you can define and instantiate objects within a single method without having the class appear outside that context.</p>
